= Ambiguity =

== Overview ==
'''Ambiguity''' occurs when a linguistic expression supports more than one distinguishable interpretation under the relevant analysis.

Ambiguity can arise because a word has multiple readings, because a sentence has more than one syntactic structure, because scope can be assigned in different ways, or because several interpretive mechanisms interact.

== Lexical ambiguity ==
A word or phrase can have multiple meanings.

For example, ''bank'' can refer to a financial institution or the side of a river.

Lexical ambiguity can involve unrelated meanings or related senses. [[Polysemy]] concerns multiple related senses and should not be treated as identical to ambiguity as a whole.

== Structural ambiguity ==
A sentence can have more than one syntactic analysis.

For example:

<blockquote>I saw the person with the telescope.</blockquote>

The phrase “with the telescope” can describe the instrument used for seeing or modify “the person.”

This is a [[Syntax|syntactic]] difference that produces an interpretive difference.

== Scope ambiguity ==
Quantifiers, negation, modals, and other operators can interact to produce multiple scope readings.

A sentence such as “Every student read a book” can allow a reading with one shared book or potentially different books.

== Ambiguity and context ==
[[Context (linguistics)|Context]] often helps a listener select an intended interpretation.

That does not mean the expression was never ambiguous. Ambiguity concerns the available interpretations; contextual disambiguation concerns how one is selected in use.

== Ambiguity and vagueness ==
Ambiguity is different from vagueness.

A vague expression may have one general meaning with unclear boundaries. An ambiguous expression has distinguishable readings.

The distinction can be difficult in practice and depends partly on linguistic analysis.

== Why ambiguity matters ==
Ambiguity matters in language understanding, lexicography, law, translation, language teaching, search, natural-language processing, and [[Meaning representation|meaning representation]].

Computational systems often need to perform some form of disambiguation before or during downstream interpretation.

== Epistemic notes ==
Not every apparent ambiguity requires separate stored lexical meanings. Theories differ over whether readings should be represented as distinct lexical entries, underspecified structures, pragmatic enrichments, constructions, or context-sensitive derivations.

